course: biology
lesson: 217
title: Worms, snails and slugs
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: After a night of rain the lawn and the pavement are full of pink earthworms, and the paths are criss-crossed with the shiny silver trails of snails and slugs.
explain: These soft little animals have no bones at all: a worm moves by squeezing and stretching its rings while tiny bristles grip the soil, and a snail glides along on a wave of slime under its one big foot.
explain: A worm has no eyes and no lungs; it breathes through its damp skin, which is why it must stay moist and dies quickly if it dries out in the sun.
explain: Worms are the gardener's best friends: they pull dead leaves down into their burrows, eat them with soil and leave rich crumbly droppings, so a field full of worms is a field full of good soil.
explain: Snails and slugs nibble our lettuces with thousands of tiny teeth, but they are food for thrushes, hedgehogs and frogs, so a garden needs them too.
fact: Earthworms often come up to the surface after rain; one likely reason is that wet ground lets them travel across the surface without drying out, but scientists are not completely sure why they do it.
fact: A worm cut in half does not become two worms; sometimes the head end survives and grows a new tail, but the tail end usually dies.
fact: An earthworm has no eyes and no lungs; it breathes through its moist skin and can sense light with its skin.
fact: Worms pull dead leaves into their burrows and eat them with soil; their droppings, called casts, make the soil rich, and their tunnels let air and water in.
fact: A worm's body is made of many rings called segments, and tiny bristles on them grip the soil while the body squeezes and stretches.
fact: A snail glides on a layer of slime that helps it grip and slide, even upside down on a leaf, and leaves a shiny trail behind.
fact: A snail hides in its shell for protection, and in dry weather or winter it can seal the opening with a door of dried slime.
fact: A garden snail has eyes at the tips of its two long tentacles, which it pulls in when they are touched; the two short tentacles below are for smell and touch.
fact: A snail eats with a ribbon covered in thousands of tiny teeth that scrapes food like a file.
fact: A slug is like a snail without an outside shell; it hides in damp, dark places by day and comes out at night or in rain.
fact: A song thrush smashes snails on a favourite stone, called its anvil, to crack the shell; broken shells pile up around it.
fact: A worm has no legs, and a snail or slug has one wide muscly foot instead of legs.
exercise: Why do earthworms often come up onto the lawn after rain? | Wet ground lets them move on the surface without drying out | They are drowning in their tunnels and come up to die | The rain washes them out of the clouds | They come up to drink the puddles before the sun dries them up
exercise: What happens if a worm is cut in half? | It does not become two worms; the tail end usually dies | Both halves always grow into two whole, healthy worms | Both halves join back together again | Nothing happens, because worms feel nothing at all and heal at once
exercise: How does an earthworm breathe? | Through its moist skin | Through a pair of tiny lungs | Through gills behind its head | Through a hole in its tail
exercise: Why must a worm keep its skin damp? | It breathes through its skin, which only works when moist | Dry skin makes its tiny eyes stop working properly | Dry skin makes its bristles fall out | A dry worm cannot find its way home through the dark soil
exercise: Why are worms good for the soil? | They eat dead leaves and leave rich droppings and air tunnels | They eat all the weeds' roots so that only flowers can grow | They keep the soil cold and dry | They frighten moles and beetles away from gardens for good
exercise: What are a worm's droppings called? | Casts | Pellets | Shells | Little seeds
exercise: How does a worm move through the soil? | Its rings squeeze and stretch while tiny bristles grip | It wriggles along on hundreds of tiny legs under its body | It rolls like a ball down its tunnel | It swims through the soil with little fins on its sides
exercise: What does the shiny trail behind a snail come from? | The slime it glides on | Water dripping from its shell | Sugar from the plants it ate | Spit it leaves to mark its way
exercise: How can a snail survive a hot dry summer? | It seals its shell with a door of dried slime | It leaves its shell and digs deep into the cool ground | It drinks enough water for the year | It grows a thicker skin of fur
exercise: Where are a garden snail's eyes? | At the tips of its two long tentacles | On the front of its shell, just above its head | Under its foot | At the tips of its two short tentacles
exercise: What are a snail's two short tentacles for? | Smelling and touching | Seeing things far away | Breathing in fresh air | Holding on to its food
exercise: How does a snail eat a lettuce leaf? | It scrapes it with a ribbon of thousands of tiny teeth | It sucks the juice out through a long hollow straw | It bites with two big front teeth | It soaks the leaf up slowly through the bottom of its foot
exercise: What is a slug? | A snail-like animal without an outside shell | A baby snail that has not grown its shell yet | A worm that has learned to climb | A snail that has lost its shell in a fight
exercise: Where do slugs usually spend the sunny daytime? | Hidden in damp, dark places | Sunbathing on top of the leaves | Flying between the plants at night | Deep in the pond under water
exercise: Why does a thrush bang a snail on a stone? | To crack the shell and eat the snail inside | To wash the slime off before playing with it | To build a nest out of the broken shells | To warn other birds away
exercise: How many legs does a snail have? | None; it moves on one big foot | Six, just like an insect has | Eight, just like a spider has | Two, hidden under the shell
test: «mate chopped a worm in half with his spade, says now he's got 2 worms lol» What is right? | No, they do not become two worms; at best the head end lives and grows a new tail | Yes, every cut worm turns into two | Yes, and the two halves will grow up to be twin worms | No, cut worms just wriggle about and join back up
test: In a cartoon a smiling worm wears glasses to read a book. What is true about real worms? | Real worms have no eyes but feel light with their skin | Real worms have two tiny blue eyes | Real worms see very well in the dark, much better than cats | Real worms have eyes on stalks, just like snails do
test: What would happen to an earthworm left on a hot, dry pavement all afternoon? | It would dry out and could not breathe through its skin | It would grow a hard shell to save itself from the heat | It would slowly turn into a slug | Nothing, worms love dry heat and lie in the sun
test: Count them: 4 garden snails crawl up a wall. How many eye-tentacles and how many feet altogether? | 8 eye-tentacles and 4 feet | 4 eye-tentacles and 4 feet | 8 eye-tentacles and 16 feet | 16 eye-tentacles and 8 feet
test: «my nan says slugs are just snails that got kicked outta their shells» Is she right? | No, slugs are a separate kind that has no outside shell | Yes, a snail that loses its shell becomes a slug | Yes, slugs find empty old shells to move into | No, slugs are really a kind of worm
test: A gardener counts 0 worms in one field and 300 in another. Which field probably has the richest soil? | The one with 300 worms | The one with no worms | Both are the same | Worms make no difference to soil
test: «worm's got loads of legs, look, it's walking» What is really helping it grip? | Tiny bristles on its rings | Hundreds of little legs | Sticky slime from its eyes | Its long sharp teeth
test: You find a pile of broken snail shells round one flat stone. What has probably happened? | A thrush has been cracking snails on its anvil | The snails all crashed into the stone in the dark | Children threw the snails there | The snails left their old shells there to grow new ones
test: What would happen if you gently touched a snail's long tentacles? | It would pull them in, because its eyes are at the tips | It would bite your finger hard | It would leave its shell behind and run away to hide | It would grow two more tentacles to protect itself
test: «bloody slugs ate all my lettuce last night, where are they now??» Where should she look in the morning? | Under pots and stones in damp, dark spots | High up in the trees with the birds | Out on the sunny path, sunbathing | Inside the lettuce leaves in the hot sun
test: Count them: a worm, a snail and a slug race on the path. How many legs is that altogether? | None | Three | Twelve | Eight
exam: Identify the principal means by which respiration is accomplished in the earthworm. | Gaseous exchange occurs through the moist surface of the skin | Air is drawn into a pair of internal lungs | Water is passed across gills situated near the head of the animal | Air is admitted through an aperture situated at the posterior end
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the consequence of bisecting an earthworm? | Two complete worms are not produced, and the posterior portion generally perishes | Two complete and independent worms are invariably produced from the halves | The two portions subsequently reunite into a single worm | The worm sustains no injury whatsoever, since it possesses no capacity for sensation
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the location of the visual organs of the garden snail. | They are situated at the extremities of the longer pair of tentacles | They are situated at the extremities of the shorter pair of tentacles | They are situated upon the anterior surface of the shell | They are situated beneath the muscular foot
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the song thrush strikes snails against a particular stone. | The shell is thereby fractured so that the soft body may be consumed | The mucus is thereby removed prior to the construction of a nest of shells | Other birds are thereby warned to vacate the territory | The snail is thereby encouraged to vacate its shell voluntarily
